@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the searches of the Yogāvacara run, 2026-09-26
#
# Four searches. Three are over one work read complete, so each is a statement
# about the whole of the Yogāvacara's Manual and not about a sample, and all
# three came back empty. The fourth is a different kind and is the one this file
# would be poorer without: a search over the compiled holding for the material
# that would refute a generalization this run was about to write, which found it
# and stopped the writing.
#
# In a file of its own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:BoranPericulumSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Yogāvacara's Manual states any caution or danger"@en, "Ob das Yogāvacara-Manual irgendeine Kautel oder Gefahr nennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Yogāvacara's Manual, the whole work — preamble, invocation, the meditation of the fivefold zest with its twelve sub-exercises, the six pairs, the meditation on happiness, the breathing exercise, the exercise by devices, the ten foul things, the bodily parts, the ten recollections, the immaterial realms, the four highest states and the ten forms of knowledge (trans. Woodward 1916, PTS Translation Series VI)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A caution addressed to the practitioner: a danger, a harm, a contraindication, a state of body or mind in which a station is not to be attempted, an instruction not to do something, a consequence of doing it wrongly. The question is put because this corpus states signs in greater number and greater detail than anything else in this holding, and a transmission that grades its stages by appearances has the most to gain from a rule about which appearances are not to be trusted."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole work read at the wording, then searched case-insensitively over the full text for: beware, danger, dangerous, harm, harmful, injure, injury, must not, should not, do not, avoid, careful, caution, forbidden, unfit, unwholesome, and for the negative forms cannot, unable, in vain, fail, fails, failed, does not appear, do not arise, no sign, wrong, mad. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "Nothing of the kind in the work. The word fail occurs some ninety-five times and every one of them is the rubric that he has without fail performed the first duties, which is a formula of completed preparation and not a diagnosis. The word danger occurs five times: once in the translator's theosophical footnote, and four times inside the similes of the ten forms of knowledge, where the dangers are the content of the insight — the forest of wild beasts, the tank of water-sprites, the snake caught by the tail — and not hazards of the practice. Must not occurs once, in a note of the translator's about a technical term. Mad occurs once, in the translator's prefatory note, in the report carried at pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako."@en ;
    skos:note "What the empty result is worth. The work is a liturgy of rubrics and every rubric is in the indicative: the element appears to him, the two images appear to him, the perception of the mental states appears. There is no conditional anywhere in it and no alternative branch. A practitioner using this manual has been told what will happen and has been given no sentence to read if it does not, which is a different thing from being given an excuse: the case is not covered, it is not raised."@en ;
    skos:note "The one caution the volume does carry is not the manual's and is held elsewhere for that reason: the translator's footnote compiled from a theosophical work of 1911, which says that arousing the centres below the navel is a process attended with great danger. It stands at pmc:WoodwardCakkaInterpolatio in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl. A run that searched the volume rather than the work would have found a caution and credited it to an eighteenth century manuscript."@en ;
    skos:note "Where a run should go next. The Siamese branch of the transmission is reported to carry what is missing here: a manual from Wat Ratchasittharam whose pīti section has been published with translation, a manual ascribed to King Taksin dated 1775, and the Khmer material edited by Bizot, of which one text is reported to state a colour condition outright — that the embryonic objects must be red like a ball of blood, and that if another colour is mixed with it the form cannot be born. If that is at the wording it is the failure condition this manual lacks, in the same transmission, and the pair would be worth more than either alone. None of it was opened in this run and nothing about it is claimed."@en .

pmp:BoranTerminusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Manual says what follows when a sign does not come"@en, "Ob das Manual sagt, was folgt, wenn ein Zeichen nicht kommt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Yogāvacara's Manual, the whole work as above (trans. Woodward 1916)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the practitioner what he is to conclude or do when a station has been held and the appearance stated for it has not arisen: a number of sittings after which he is to go back, a rule for repeating a station, a state that would show the station was not reached, a false or counterfeit appearance, an obstacle of the kind the Theravāda commentary names as the ten imperfections of insight."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "The whole work read at the wording, then searched for: again, repeat, once more, if not, otherwise, until, counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, imperfection, obstacle, hindrance, defilement, upakkilesa, and for every numeral and every occurrence of times, days and hours in the rubrics. Every hit read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "Nothing. The work has three quantified things and none of them is a term of that kind. The wax-taper clock measures how long a station lasts and its eighth stick is the token that the forward run is complete, which says when to stop meditating and nothing about whether anything came. The count in the breathing exercise — not less than five times nor more than ten — is a closed count with no provision for the tenth repetition passing empty, carried at pmc:BoranPancaDasaVara. The extension ladder measures distance. Repeat and again occur only in the rubric that the station is to be run in reverse order."@en ;
    skos:note "The words counterfeit, false, illusion, deception, obstacle, hindrance and defilement do not occur in the manual at all. Imperfection occurs once and it is the translator's gloss on a term, not a state of the practice. That is the finding this run was looking for and the reason the protocol at examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl was opened: the transmission the Visuddhimagga's ten imperfections belong to has a meditation manual of its own in which no imperfection is named and the appearances are the whole of the criterion."@en ;
    skos:note "One qualification on the negative, stated because it is the honest boundary of a search in a translation. The manual is a liturgy whose repetitions the printed text abbreviates with et cetera, and the elided parts are given in full earlier on the same page or the page before, as the translator's own footnote says. A condition of failure hidden inside an elided repetition is therefore possible in principle, and it is very unlikely: the elisions are of the request formula and the posture rubric, both of which are printed in full more than once, and a condition would have had to stand in a passage the editors chose to abbreviate as identical to another."@en .

pmp:BoranCustosForisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Manual names anybody who stays outside the absorption"@en, "Ob das Manual jemanden nennt, der außerhalb der Vertiefung bleibt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Yogāvacara's Manual, the whole work as above (trans. Woodward 1916), and the translator's prefatory note of the same volume for the comparison."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A second person on whom the outcome of the operation is made to depend: a teacher who watches, an attendant who reads a sign the practitioner cannot read, a witness, anybody at all who is required to be present. The question is put because the holding carries pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState, which holds that a transmission whose practitioner enters a condition where he can no longer check the operation names such a person and states that without him it does not come off; and this practice puts its practitioner in exactly that condition, with a clock made audible because he cannot see it."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Read at the wording and searched for: sir, bhante, confessor, teacher, guru, guide, master, pupil, sisya, attendant, companion, alone, solitary, and every occurrence of the formula Give leave and of okāsa."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:BoranOkasaBhante ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "The search found material that runs against the expectation rather than a plain absence, which is why the outcome is the third value and not the first. One second person is named: a bhante addressed three times for pardon at the opening of the fivefold zest. His office then ends — the editor of 1916 states that the confessor's function ends at the foot of that page — and the recurring okāsa afterwards is, on her reading, the solitary adjuring himself. The preamble has the practitioner go to a solitary forest, the foot of a tree, or a lonely place far from the haunts of men. So the work names the person the statement predicts, at the door, and then sends him away and prescribes solitude."@en ;
    skos:note "Not entered as a counter-case to pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState and the reason is in that statement's second limb. It provides for the transmission that binds the effect to something the practitioner can check on himself while he does it — an exact wording, a count, a prescribed hour — and says that such a transmission names no such person and may require him to be alone. This work has all three: a fixed request formula, a count of five to ten, and a candle clock he can hear. So it falls on the statement's own other side and confirms rather than opposes it. What the search establishes is that it falls there, which was not obvious from a corpus whose practitioner has his eyes shut."@en ;
    skos:note "The living transmission, on the other hand, supplies exactly the figure the manual does not. The report at pmc:BoranSisyaUmmattako holds that the practice must not be taught by a guru who is not fully versed in it and able to see clairvoyantly what effect each meditation is having on his pupil, and names a pupil who went mad and died. That is a watcher outside the state, with the outcome made to depend on him, stated by the last people to hold the practice and not by the book they held. The split between a manual that prescribes solitude and a lineage that requires a watcher is the finding of this search."@en .

pmp:ColorisLocusHypothesisSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the failure condition follows where the colour appears"@en, "Ob die Misslingensbedingung dem Ort der Farbe folgt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The compiled holding of this graph as of 2026-09-26, every node whose definition names a colour, read at the node; and the wording of the two works read in this batch, the Turba philosophorum and the Yogāvacara's Manual."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Material that would refute a statement this run had drafted and did not write: that where an operative transmission grades the stages of its own operation by a sequence of colours, whether it states a condition under which a stage counts as not reached follows where the colour appears — a transmission whose colours appear in a material outside the practitioner stating such a condition, and one whose colours appear within the practitioner stating the sequence alone. The counter-search required by SPEC §16 before a generalization is written, run against the holding as that section requires and not against the literature."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Query over the compiled graph for every node whose skos:definition contains colour or color, which returned thirty-six; each read at the node. Then the nodes returned were sorted by whether the colour appears in a material or in the practitioner, and those in the second class were read for any caution, criterion, term or excuse standing beside them."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:BoranPancajhanaVanna ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-26" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-26" ;
    skos:note "The refuting case is pmp:SteinerUebungAmSamen in traditions/anthroposophy.ttl and it was found on the first pass. Its colours are inner and expressly not seen as physical eyes see colours — a flame-formation felt as lilac-blue in the middle and bluish at the edge from the seed, greenish-blue and yellowish-red from the dying plant — and they are announced in advance in the same sentence as the instruction, which is the condition the drafted statement made its whole point of. And the same chapter states what the drafted statement said such a transmission does not: that many will take it for illusion and some will fall away, that everything then depends on having the courage to press forward, and that the pupil must continually cultivate the sense which distinguishes truth from deception and examine again and again whether he has fallen out of his equilibrium. Two cautions in the holding already point at that practice. So an inner colour sequence can carry a caution, an authenticity test and an operator-located excuse, and the location of the colour does not decide anything."@en ;
    skos:note "A second case bounds it from the other side and was inspected in the same pass. pmc:NeidanZhengyan grades by an announced sequence of appearances, several of them colours, and the same work carries its failure side in the adjacent chapter together with the most exactly quantified tolerance in this holding at pmc:NeidanSanwanke. It is not a counter-case to the drafted wording, because its sequence is graded by events rather than by a colour scale, but it shows that the class the drafted statement was carving out would have contained two texts and excluded everything that looks like them."@en ;
    skos:note "The statement was therefore not written, and this node is what the run has instead. That is the point of recording it: a hypothesis refused before it was entered leaves nothing in the holding unless the refusal is entered, and the next run would form the same hypothesis from the same two works. The one thing that does survive the search is not a generalization but a single case, and it is carried as a protocol: this manual grades by inner appearances and names no counterfeit, which refutes pmc:TheSignTakenForTheAttainment. See examinations/the-sign-and-the-counterfeit.ttl."@en ;
    skos:note "What the search says about the method and not about the colours. The drafted statement was formed from the two works read in one batch, which is the exact situation SPEC §16 warns about: whoever generalizes from what he has just read has read the class the evidence comes from and not the class the counter-case lies in. The counter-case here came from a tradition file opened four days earlier by another run, and one query over the compiled graph returned it."@en .
